From: MahanHD <mahanheidari2003@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: don't shmem-truncate a VRAM carveout object on purge

When the display has no IOMMU every buffer is a VRAM carveout object,
made with drm_gem_private_object_init() and without a shmem file. Purging
one still went on to shmem_truncate_range(file_inode(obj->filp), ...)
after put_pages() had handed its range back to the carveout, and
file_inode(NULL) faulted inside the shrinker, killing kswapd the first
time memory got tight while a DONTNEED buffer was cached.

Stop after the purge's common part when there is no shmem file.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c	2026-10-15 09:54:09.791506085 +0400
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c	2026-10-15 09:54:09.807164107 +0400
@@ -873,6 +873,18 @@
 
 	drm_gem_free_mmap_offset(obj);
 
+	/*
+	 * A VRAM carveout object - what every buffer is when the display has
+	 * no IOMMU - is made with drm_gem_private_object_init() and has no
+	 * shmem file: put_pages() above has already handed its range back to
+	 * the carveout, and there is nothing for shmem to drop. Without this
+	 * check file_inode(NULL) faults at offsetof(struct file, f_inode) from
+	 * inside the shrinker, i.e. in kswapd, the first time memory gets
+	 * tight while a DONTNEED buffer is cached.
+	 */
+	if (!obj->filp)
+		return;
+
 	/* Our goal here is to return as much of the memory as
 	 * is possible back to the system as we are called from OOM.
 	 * To do this we must instruct the shmfs to drop all of its
